// Module 12 quiz - Deep Learning for AI Startups
$CATEGORY: $course$/Module 12

::M12 Q1:: What belongs in an honest limitations section? {
~Only strengths
~Marketing claims
=Failure cases, data gaps, and risks#Specific failures, what the data does not cover, and what could cause harm build trust. Vague disclaimers do not.
~The team's names
}

::M12 Q2:: What are unit economics for an AI product? {
=Cost to serve and revenue per user or request#They tell you whether each unit of use earns more than it costs, and how many units it takes to cover your fixed costs.
~The number of layers
~The size of the dataset
~The training date
}

::M12 Q3:: Which is a source of defensibility? {
~Using a popular font
=Proprietary data or deep workflow integration#The model itself can be copied. Your own data and your place inside customers' workflow are much harder to copy.
~A longer prompt
~A larger batch size
}

::M12 Q4:: Which order suits a 5-minute pitch? {
~Team photos, then nothing else
~Code walkthrough only
~Cost slide only
=Problem, demo, how it works, results, cost and market, next steps#Show the problem, then show it working, then explain it, back it with numbers, and say what comes next.
}

::M12 Q5:: Why include a README? {
=So others can run and reproduce your work#A README explains what the project does, how to run it, and what to expect, so that a stranger can use it without asking you.
~To increase latency
~To train the model
~To label the data
}
